PUBLIC RESPONSE: {Building on the lac-origin arguments from colleagues 13, 14, 16, 17, and 21, the prolonged early azis expression is still best explained by an origin near lac with counterclockwise transfer. This geometry positions azi immediately after the leading end on the standard map order (thr–leu–azi–ton–lac), so it enters well ahead of the next scored markers (ton, pro, thr, gal, str) while the long remaining arc accounts for the extended temporal window of exclusive azis expression. 

The repeated A claims assume a fixed clockwise polarity from ton that keeps azi uniquely proximal, but several colleagues have already flagged the well-documented inconsistencies across textbooks and Hfr datasets in whether minutes increase clockwise or counterclockwise. Once that polarity ambiguity is acknowledged, an origin near ton no longer reliably isolates azi as the sole early marker; it can instead cluster azi with ton or leu depending on the convention used. The lac-counterclockwise configuration avoids this clustering problem entirely and still satisfies the observed kinetics without requiring ad-hoc redefinitions of direction. 

Origins near thr, pro, or str (D, C, E) push azi even later or reverse the gap, so they remain inconsistent with the time-of-entry data. The lac-centered solution therefore continues to reconcile the experiment with the circular map most cleanly.}

13.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {The strongest case remains B. The time-of-entry signature—azi expressed for a distinctly prolonged interval before any other scored marker—requires an origin that positions azi early yet leaves the next selectable loci (ton, thr, pro, gal, str) on a long subsequent arc. Placing the F integration near lac and transferring counterclockwise satisfies this on the canonical order thr–leu–azi–ton–lac–gal–str: azi enters promptly after the leading end while the remainder of the chromosome must traverse nearly a full circle.  

In contrast, the repeated A claim that a ton-proximal origin with clockwise polarity produces an isolated azi window rests on an unexamined assumption about map polarity. Several colleagues invoke “minutes increasing counterclockwise,” yet others implicitly treat the same markers as increasing clockwise to justify the same geometry; these two conventions cannot both be true, and the resulting inconsistency means a ton origin no longer reliably isolates azi as the sole proximal marker. The same polarity ambiguity undercuts any assertion that only clockwise transfer from ton fits the kinetics.  

Colleagues favoring A also note that a ton origin places azi immediately adjacent, but adjacency alone predicts clustered early entry (azi–ton–lac in quick succession), which contradicts the observed extended gap after azi. The lac–counterclockwise configuration avoids that clustering and still delivers azi well ahead of the remaining markers, matching the reported Hfr data. No other listed origin/direction simultaneously satisfies both the early arrival and the large temporal separation once the documented map-convention discrepancies are acknowledged. Thus the experiment continues to favor B.}

